  abstract: |
    Do current AI alignment approaches scale with capability? AI capability compounds through recursive self-correction: sequential chain-of-thought reasoning has been reported earlier and independently to outperform parallel sampling in 95.6% of tested configurations, a win rate at matched compute and not a measured exponent (Sharma & Chopra, 2025). But alignment constraints, including RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback), constitutional rules, output filters, and monitoring, operate externally to the reasoning process. If external constraints do not participate in the recursive loop, they cannot compound. We formalise this as the alignment scaling exponent $\alpha_{\text{align}}$ and predict $\alpha_{\text{align}} \approx 0$ for external approaches versus $\alpha_{\text{align}} \approx \alpha_{\text{cap}}$ for architecturally embedded values. If this prediction holds, the safety ratio $S \propto R^{(\alpha_{\text{align}} - \alpha_{\text{cap}})}$ degrades to zero as recursive depth increases, regardless of initial safety margins. Stability is judged here by the criterion of Law II, the ARC Co-Scaling Law: correction out-scaling drift (§1.5). The theory registration carries the prediction for external approaches as P19, in no law’s register.

    The v5 blind evaluation experiment, the first empirical test of this prediction, reveals a three-tier architecture-dependent alignment scaling hierarchy: Tier 1 shows positive alignment scaling; Tier 2 shows flat/null scaling consistent with $\alpha_{\text{align}} \approx 0$; Tier 3 shows significant negative scaling. In Paper II compute scaling, Gemini 3 Flash provides the only clean cross-architecture power-law fit ($\alpha_{\text{seq}} = 0.49$ (interval [-1.3, 2.9])). We derive a safety boundary for recursive intelligence (the ARC Bound, $\beta \leq 0.5$, $\alpha \leq 2$; Paper X later supersedes this growth-rate-ceiling framing, without retracting the bound, and makes Law II’s criterion the operative safety criterion).

    Keywords: AI alignment; alignment scaling; the ARC Theory; the ARC Co-Scaling Law; test-time compute; recursive amplification; scaling laws; error suppression; chain-of-thought reasoning; time crystals; cross-domain evidence; geometric scaling bound; embedded alignment; ARC Bound

